{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01URWS4QLDM8wLa8h6D4a5aL", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sokushinbutsu\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001562124.pdf\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001547240.docx\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.thetvdb.com/series/a-fortune-teller-told-me/episodes/8530329\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/sokushinbutsu-mummies-japan-buddhist-monks\"}, {\"url\": \"https://terebess.hu/zen/mesterek/harada.html\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.tokyoweekender.com/art_and_culture/japanese-culture/5-fascinating-monks-japanese-history/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001562122.pdf\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.worldhistory.org/audioplayer/en/1-15987/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.tokyoweekender.com/?p=147561\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:35:47.374Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "fortune teller would not live past forty left Tōdaiji Kasagi cave ascetic monk", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_014Gq5xDBSvHkc4BsjJnx3GR", "content": [{"title": "Sokushinbutsu", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sokushinbutsu"}, {"title": "www.mlit.go.jp", "url": "https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001562124.pdf"}, {"title": "www.mlit.go.jp", "url": "https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001547240.docx"}, {"title": "www.thetvdb.com", "url": "https://www.thetvdb.com/series/a-fortune-teller-told-me/episodes/8530329"}, {"title": "Why did these monks in Japan choose to mummify themselves?", "url": "https://www.nationalgeographic.com/history/article/sokushinbutsu-mummies-japan-buddhist-monks"}, {"title": "ZEN MESTEREK ZEN MASTERS", "url": "https://terebess.hu/zen/mesterek/harada.html"}, {"title": "1. Benkei – The Giant Warrior Monk", "url": "https://www.tokyoweekender.com/art_and_culture/japanese-culture/5-fascinating-monks-japanese-history/"}, {"title": "www.mlit.go.jp", "url": "https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/common/001562122.pdf"}, {"title": "www.worldhistory.org", "url": "https://www.worldhistory.org/audioplayer/en/1-15987/"}, {"title": "1. Benkei – The Giant Warrior Monk", "url": "https://www.tokyoweekender.com/?p=147561"}]}, "The search didn't turn up the story you're describing. None of the results mention Tōdaiji, Kasagi cave, or a fortune teller's prediction about a monk dying before forty.\n\nThe closest match is a personal account by the Zen teacher Harada Sogaku. He was told as a youth by a previous generation of fortune-tellers that he was supposed to live to fifty-five, and he credits his longer life to zazen. That's a different story, but it involves a fortune-teller's lifespan prediction and a Buddhist practitioner.\n\nThe results also cover sokushinbutsu, the practice of Japanese Buddhist monks who observed asceticism to the point of death and entered mummification while still alive. That is the general background for the ascetic-monk angle, but nothing connects it to Kasagi or Tōdaiji.\n\nA search with the Japanese names, such as 笠置 (Kasagi) and 東大寺 (Tōdaiji) with 占い師 (fortune teller), or the name of the specific monk if you know it, might find the source."], "durationSeconds": 4.979006416, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01URWS4QLDM8wLa8h6D4a5aL", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
